<article class="env-proposition" id="067G">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/067G">Proposition <span data-tag="067G">9.3.2.6</span>.</a> Let $\kappa \leq \lambda $ be regular cardinals, where $\lambda $ is uncountable, let $f: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as an $\operatorname{Ind}_{\kappa }^{\lambda }$-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by the $(\kappa ,\lambda )$-compact objects. Then $h$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$ as an idempotent-completion of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      It follows from Proposition <a data-tag="0653" href="/tag/0653">9.3.2.3</a> that $h$ induces an equivalence from $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to a full subcategory of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1 \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$ which generates $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ under $\lambda $-small $\kappa $-filtered colimits. Applying Lemma <a data-tag="067F" href="/tag/067F">9.3.2.5</a>, we see that every object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$ is a retract of an object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_1$. It will therefore suffice to show that $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$ is idempotent-complete. This follows from Proposition <a data-tag="040B" href="/tag/040B">8.5.4.10</a>, since the $\infty $-category $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ is idempotent-complete (Proposition <a data-tag="062R" href="/tag/062R">9.2.1.14</a>) and the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}_0$ is closed under retracts (Remark <a data-tag="064Z" href="/tag/064Z">9.2.5.18</a>). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
